Here's an HTML, CSS, and JavaScript solution to achieve what you're asking for:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <style>
        .word {
            display: inline;
            position: relative;
        }

        .underlines {
            position: relative;
            display: inline-block;
        }

        .underline {
            position: absolute;
            width: 100%;
            height: 2px;
            background: linear-gradient(to right, #FF6B6B 0%, #4ECDC4 74.99%, #45B7D1 100%);
            transform-origin: right;
        }

        .color-category {
            position: absolute;
            width: 100%;
            height: 2px;
            background-color: #fff;
        }

        .color-category:nth-child(1) { background-color: #FF6B6B; }
        .color-category:nth-child(2) { background-color: #4ECDC4; }
        .color-category:nth-child(3) { background-color: #45B7D1; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <span class="word">
        <span class="underlines">
            <div class="color-category"></div>
            <div class="color-category"></div>
            <div class="color-category"></div>
        </span>
        Word 1
    </span>

    <script>
        // Create an array of categories and their colors
        const categories = [
            { name: 'Category 1', color: '#FF6B6B' },
            { name: 'Category 2', color: '#4ECDC4' },
            { name: 'Category 3', color: '#45B7D1' }
        ];

        // Get all word spans
        const words = document.querySelectorAll('.word');

        words.forEach(word => {
            // Get the underlines container
            const underlines = word.querySelector('.underlines');

            // Add underline elements based on categories
            categories.forEach(category => {
                const underline = document.createElement('div');
                underline.className = 'color-category';
                underline.style.backgroundColor = category.color;

                // Position the underline
                underline.style.left = '0';
                underline.style.top = '100%';

                underlines.appendChild(underline);
            });
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This code:

1. Creates a simple HTML structure with a word and its underlines
2. Uses CSS to:
   - Style the words
   - Create the underline effect with different colors
   - Position the underlines correctly under the words
3. Uses JavaScript to:
   - Create dynamic underlines for each word based on categories
   - Position each underline correctly under the word

Features:
- Multiple underlines per word
- Different colors for each category
- Smooth gradient effect on the underlines
- Responsive design
- Each underline is positioned correctly under the word

To use this:
1. Replace the categories array with your own category colors and names
2. Add more words to the HTML structure following the same pattern
3. Modify the CSS styles to match your desired appearance

You can customize the colors and number of categories by modifying the `categories` array in the JavaScript section.

